h an open court and fountain in the middle): an Italian Renaissance palazzo of pink-grey Milford granite on a base course, one tall storey of windowless galleries round the open central court with its fountain and glazed loggia, the three-arched entrance loggia and steps on the Mall front, cornice and balustrade, the galleries' low glass roofs behind it; to the east, the Arthur M. Sackler Gallery's granite entrance pavilion (1987) under its small pyramidal roofs in the Enid A. Haupt Garden (the gallery itself is underground). Guessed: heights, bays, roof lights, the pavilion's size and place. No photographs were available. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs: the footprint already sits on the outline (the outline would only shift it a cell either way, within the steps and terraces), so it stays; the Milford pink granite is now drawn paler, nearer its light pink-grey, over a darker base course, and the walls have a row of tall upper-storey windows on every side (their count and rhythm are guessed; the Mall front keeps its three-arched loggia between them); the Sackler's pavilion is in the same granite. Still guessed: the exact window pattern, the skylights' layout, the courtyard planting."}